  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nBehind the exiting cold front, morning valley fog will \ndissipate. High pressure eases into the Northeast through the \nmorning, sagging south across the Mid-Atlantic by the late \nafternoon. This will bring a very pleasant day with mostly sunny\nskies and light winds up to 12 mph. There is plenty of dry air \naloft, but a sharper inversion atop the boundary layer and light\nwinds should prevent this drier air from mixing down.\n\nClouds may begin to thicken aloft come Saturday evening and \novernight. Broad low pressure to the north will help advect \nmoisture into the region from the west as the high's influence \nweakens. Mid level jet will be poised across the St Lawrence \nValley, and the entrance region will near the US/CAN border by \nSunday afternoon. Added moisture and lift will bring the chance \nfor showers into the evening. For now this is focused across the\nnorth as a front drops south, with uncertainty on coverage \nsouth of the foothills into the overnight period. Some \ninstability may be available for thunderstorms, but greater \nconfidence rests with a chance of showers.\n\nUnder unidirectional NW flow, dry air flushes back in Monday \nfor another dry and mostly sunny day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nFast WNW 500 mb flow overspreads the Northeast U.S., on the \nperiphery of a strong ridge to our south. It will be a favorable \nsetup for disturbances to ride the ridge and bring some rain chances \nto New England next week. At the surface, should see a stationary \nfront setup in our vicinity Tuesday. This feature may lift \nnortheast into our region as a warm front with low-level \nsoutherly flow and moisture, before a cold front on Thursday \nbrings drier air back with winds turning westerly.\n\nPrecipitation looks more hit-or-miss on Tuesday, before widespread \nrains arrive Wednesday. The more widespread chance for rain on \nWednesday-early Thursday will be with a surface low moving through \nthe flow. Most ensemble members have it tracking right over New \nEngland. One key factor to watch will be the warm front position, \nwhich will dictate if it is a more stratiform or convective rain. \nThe ECMWF brings the warm front north into our region, while the GFS \nkeeps it well southwest. This also will have an impact on \ntemperatures, which for now are low confidence, but leaning towards \na low diurnal range with the clouds/rain.\n\nRain chances drop from Thursday onward with drier northwest \nlow-level winds developing behind a cold front. Cannot rule out\nan isolated rain chance from a shortwave rounding the ridge \nlate week, but for now ensembles suggest rain-free conditions \nare more likely.",
